            <h3>Packaging Material Costs</h3>
            <p>Aerosol systems are generally the most expensive to package. They require high-pressure metal cans (aluminum or tinplate), specialized valves, and custom actuators. In contrast, pump and trigger sprays use standard plastic or glass bottles with threaded necks. While high-end "fine mist" pump heads can be pricey, the overall component cost for a manual spray system is typically 30-50% lower than an equivalent aerosol system, providing better margins for early-stage ventures.</p>

            <h3>Filling Line Complexity and MOQs</h3>
            <p>Filling aerosols is a specialized process that requires explosion-proof facilities and high-precision equipment to handle propellants safely. Consequently, contract manufacturers (CMOs) for aerosols often have higher MOQs—sometimes starting at 10,000 to 25,000 units. Pump and trigger sprays can be filled on much simpler liquid filling lines, which are more common and often accessible with MOQs as low as 1,000 to 5,000 units. This lower barrier to entry makes manual sprays highly attractive for "Minimum Viable Product" (MVP) launches.</p>

            <h3>Supply Chain and Hazmat Logistics</h3>
            <p>Founders often overlook the "hidden costs" of logistics. Aerosols are classified as "Dangerous Goods" (UN1950) due to their pressurized and often flammable nature. This leads to higher shipping rates, specialized warehousing requirements, and strict limitations on air freight. Manual sprays (non-pressurized) are significantly easier and cheaper to ship, especially for Direct-to-Consumer (DTC) brands relying on standard courier services like FedEx or DHL.</p>
        </section>

